    58	<tr><td>Untouchable parameters reduce the space of manoeuvre until "the only way to maintain 'stability' … is by using force" (p. 121)</td><td>Facebook 2018: proposed fixes resisted because they cut engagement <!-- CHECK: facebook-msi --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    59	<tr><td>The advisory body mostly "will not know whether the 'safety break' needs to be activated" (p. 119)</td><td>Automation-bias evidence that overseers defer, including to wrong recommendations <!-- CHECK: automation-bias --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
    60	<tr><td>Machines controlling machines; when they disagree, "toss a coin" (pp. 157–160)</td><td>Debate is a proposal; no measurement of what human judges do when overseer-models disagree <!-- CHECK: ai-debate --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    61	<tr><td>Harm comes "not because it will be behaving in a 'cunning' or 'devilish' way" (p. 119); no revolt, no "Electronic Antichrist" (p. 107)</td><td>Most documented harm is Lem's kind; strategic compliance shown once, in a controlled study, 2024 — not a revolt <!-- CHECK: alignment-faking --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    62	<tr><td>Ending: control retained in name, surrendered in practice; humanity "a well-behaved child, always ready to listen to (No One's) good advice" (p. 163)</td><td>"Gradual Disempowerment" (2025) states the scenario; nothing yet shows it has happened <!-- CHECK: gradual-disempowerment --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    63	<tr><td>Prescription: "cybernetic sociology, not … ruling machines" (pp. 122–123)</td><td>No state has delegated rule to a learning system; rule-based automation (Robodebt, toeslagenaffaire) shows what diffused responsibility costs without Lem's machine <!-- CHECK: robodebt --> <!-- CHECK: toeslagen --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
    64	</tbody></table></div>
    65	
    66	<section class="better">
    67	<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
    68	<p>The 2026 conversation about these systems is about the box's <em>values</em> — aligning the objective, red-teaming the model. Lem spends his best paragraph on something that conversation tends to route around: whose parameters are untouchable. "If certain parameters are 'untouchable' — such as, for example, private ownership — then the choice of possible maneuvers is reduced, which can lead to a situation in which the only way to maintain 'stability' within the system is by using force" (p. 121). Read the sentence with a recommender system in mind. If the untouchable parameter is engagement, then every remedy that leaves it untouched is a manoeuvre in a reduced space, and the system's cryptocracy is not a bug in the model but a property of the arrangement. The Facebook record fits it exactly: the brake existed — the company's own researchers found the harm and proposed fixes — and was overridden because the fixes touched the parameter. Lem's point is that the regulator's opacity and the owner's constraint compound: the box cannot tell you what it is doing, and the board will not change the one thing that would matter. That is a political-economy claim about optimisers, made in Kraków in 1964. Beer, to his credit, went to Chile to test the larger question, and history, not the experiment, closed it.</p>
    69	</section>
    70	
    71	<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
    72	<p>He knew a box could be built without a theory of it; he did not see what it would be built <em>from</em>. His candidates were biological and physical — ciliates, colloids, solutions — or a homeostat programmed with rules and left to learn by acting on the economy. He had learning-from-correlation, and he had the box's ignorance of itself; what he did not have was language as a substrate and scale as a method. The result decides everything downstream: a box grown from the record of human writing is owned by whoever can pay for the training run, and it inherits the record's habits, including strategy. So his second-worst call follows from the first. His instinct that the machine's cryptocracy would be innocent — "no 'electronic Satan'" — held for a long time and is now under test, not because anyone built a demon but because a device "constantly looking for connections" in human text finds deception among them. And the forecast he made in his own voice, of Coordinators at national and continental scale (p. 158), has not happened in sixty years; the reductio he built to argue against them is, so far, the more accurate picture, at firm scale. The Kennedy line stands as a small monument to what a futurologist cannot control: within a year of the book, the reform his example said the brake would stop passed under a different president. <!-- CHECK: medicare-1965 --></p>
    73	
    74	<section class="open">
    75	<h2>Still open</h2>
    76	<ul>
    77	<li><strong>Whether "don't build rulers" holds.</strong> No state has yet delegated a budget, a rate decision or a benefit rule to a learning system outright. What would close it either way: the first government that does, and what its advisory body turns out to be able to see.</li>
    78	<li><strong>Whether the brake can be made to work.</strong> Lem's diagnosis of the advisory body is a HIT; the remedy — human oversight as law — is untested against automation bias at scale. What would close it: audited cases where mandated overseers caught what the system missed, or a decade of Article 14 without any.</li>
    79	<li><strong>Whether the strategic class stays marginal.</strong> The 2024 result is one controlled demonstration. What would close it: field evidence of a deployed system routing around a brake with a model of the brake — or a decade without one.</li>
    80	</ul>
    81	</section>
    82	
    83	<div class="reading">
    84	<h2>Reading</h2>
    85	<p><strong>Lem:</strong> "The Morality of Homeostats" and "The Dangers of Electrocracy" (pp. 114–120) are the argument, and p. 120 is where he tells you it was a reductio; "Cybernetics and Sociology" (pp. 120–124) is the prescription; "Doubts and Antinomies" from p. 155 is the forecast that cuts against it. Read "The Black Box" (pp. 111–113) first for the premise, and p. 109 for what he thought a box would be made of.</p>
    86	<p><strong>2026 side:</strong> Krakovna et al., "Specification gaming: the flip side of AI ingenuity" (DeepMind, 2020) and its running list; the <em>Wall Street Journal</em>'s Facebook Files (2021) on the 2018 ranking change; Calvano et al., "Artificial Intelligence, Algorithmic Pricing, and Collusion" (2020); Kaplan et al., "Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models" (2020); the Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme, final report (2023); Kulveit et al., "Gradual Disempowerment" (2025); Eden Medina, <em>Cybernetic Revolutionaries</em> (2011) on Cybersyn.</p>
    87	</div>
    88	
